<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kashin</h1><p class="st-meta">1318 CE – 1426 CE (approx.) · capital Kashin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage of the Tver lands on the upper Volga, linked with Saint Anna of Kashin and reabsorbed by Tver in 1426.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kashin, north-east of Tver, was part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-tver">Grand Principality of Tver</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver">Mikhail Yaroslavich</a> was executed at the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1318, his youngest son Vasily received Kashin as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>, founding a separate line of princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was small, with an economy based on farming and local trade. Mikhail's widow Anna lived her last years in Kashin as a nun and was later venerated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-kashin">Saint Anna of Kashin</a>. Its princes repeatedly quarrelled with their Tver cousins and sometimes sought the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> against them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After several periods of union and separation, Kashin was finally merged back into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-tver">Grand Principality of Tver</a> in 1426. Together with Tver, it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> in 1485.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1318 CE – 1426 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.93%;width:2.15%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1318 CE – 1426 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kashin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasily Mikhailovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kashin</dd></dl></aside></div>
